<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 2: Multilevel Data                                       -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens conceptually with Simpson's paradox, then moves into R.    -->
<!--  The Berkeley admissions case is real and properly citable        -->
<!--  (Bickel, Hammel & O'Connell 1975), as is the kidney stone case    -->
<!--  (Bonovas & Piovani 2023). The Kenya tie-in (ss-kenya-relevance)  -->
<!--  is also a real, sourced fact: KUCCPS' own affirmative action     -->
<!--  criteria, not an invented case study.                            -->
<!--  The R section (sec-mics-multilevel) uses the Kenya MICS files    -->
<!--  (hh, hl, wm, ch), linked by HID, to show linking, orphan records, -->
<!--  level mapping, and what goes wrong when levels are ignored.      -->
<!--  Outstanding: kidney stone numbers/graphs, the STACK question,    -->
<!--  and the "Then more here" placeholder in ss-multilevel-structures. -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-multilevel-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Multilevel Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-multidimensional-data">
    <p>
      Most datasets contain more than one kind of thing.
    </p>

    <p>
      We start with a hospital dataset.
      This dataset records patients, but those patients are admitted to wards,
      and those wards belong to hospitals.
      A school survey collects responses from students, who sit in classrooms,
      in schools, in districts.
      Even the flamingos from <xref ref="ch-intro"/> belong to flocks, living on
      different lakes.
    </p>

    <p>
      When data has this kind of layered structure, that structure really
      matters.
      It changes which questions you can ask, which comparisons make sense, and
      what you're allowed to conclude.
      Ignore it, and you can end up with results where every calculation is
      right and the conclusion is still wrong.
      One of the examples in this chapter shows that really dramatically.
    </p>

    <p>
      So this chapter is about learning to spot that structure before you start
      any analysis.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Berkeley                                               -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-berkeley-admissions">
    <title>A Question About Fairness</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the early 1970s, people in the United States were increasingly
        worried about gender equality in higher education.
        Then some admissions data from the University of California, Berkeley,
        came along that seemed to confirm those fears.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-the-numbers">
      <title>The numbers seemed clear</title>

      <p>
        In 1973, UC Berkeley faced a lawsuit over sex bias.
        Across the whole university, 44.3% of men who applied for graduate
        programmes were accepted.
        For women, it was only 34.6%.
        Same institution, same year.
        The newspapers picked it up, and it really did look like women were
        being treated unfairly.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-berkeley-overall-admissions">
        <caption>
          University-wide graduate admission rates at UC Berkeley in 1973, by
          sex: 44.3% of male applicants were admitted, compared with 34.6% of
          female applicants.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/berkeley_img_1.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart with two bars comparing overall graduate admission
              rates at UC Berkeley in 1973.
              The bar for male applicants reaches 44.3%, noticeably taller than
              the bar for female applicants, which reaches 34.6%.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        But before anyone acted on it, researchers went back to the numbers.
        And instead of just asking <em>what</em> the numbers showed, they asked
        <em>how</em> the admissions decisions were actually made.
        We love this question, because it's the one that so often gets skipped.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-how-decisions-are-made">
      <title>One university, many decisions</title>

      <p>
        At Berkeley, there wasn't one central committee deciding who got in.
        Each department made its own decisions, with its own standards, its own
        number of places, and its own level of competition.
      </p>

      <p>
        The university-wide figures were correct.
        They just weren't the whole story.
        So let's take exactly the same data, and this time look at it department
        by department.
        Were women still less likely to get in?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-berkeley-admissions-by-dept">
        <caption>
          Graduate admission rates at UC Berkeley in 1973, by department and
          sex.
          The rates for male and female applicants vary by department.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/berkeley_img_by_dept.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart with two bars for each department comparing graduate
              admission rates at UC Berkeley in 1973.
              The bar for male applicants reaches a certain percentage,
              noticeably taller or shorter than the bar for female applicants,
              depending on the department.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-what-they-found">
      <title>What they found</title>

      <p>
        In most departments, women were admitted at about the same rate as men.
        In several, women were actually admitted at a <em>higher</em> rate!
        The discrimination that looked so obvious in the overall numbers had
        pretty much disappeared.
      </p>

      <p>
        So what's going on?
        How can women have a lower acceptance rate overall, when they weren't
        being disadvantaged in the individual departments?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-berkeley-admissions-total-applicants-by-dept">
        <caption>
          Number of graduate applicants at UC Berkeley in 1973, by department
          and sex.
          The numbers for male and female applicants vary by department.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/berkeley_img_total_applicants.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart with two bars for each department comparing the number
              of graduate applicants at UC Berkeley in 1973.
              The bar for male applicants reaches a certain number, noticeably
              taller or shorter than the bar for female applicants, depending on
              the department.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        It all comes down to where people applied.
        On average, women tended to apply to the more competitive departments:
        the ones with fewer places, where acceptance rates were low for
        everyone, whatever their sex.
        Men were more likely to apply to departments that accepted a higher
        proportion of applicants.
        When you blend all the departments into one set of university-wide
        numbers, you lose sight of <em>where people applied</em>, and all you're
        left with is a difference in <em>who got in</em>.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-berkeley-lesson">
        <title>The bias was in how the numbers were combined</title>

        <p>
          By looking at the whole university at once, very different situations
          were treated as if they were the same.
          Two separate questions got blurred together: how applicants were
          treated <em>within</em> a department, and how departments differed
          <em>from one another</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          There was nothing wrong with the arithmetic - the mistake was in how
          the situation was understood.
          Applicants belonged to departments, and departments belonged to a
          university.
          Squash those layers into one set of averages, and you throw away
          exactly the context you need to read the numbers properly.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-name-and-stakes">
      <title>Simpson's paradox</title>

      <p>
        This pattern has a name: <term>Simpson's paradox</term>
        <idx><h>Simpson's paradox</h></idx>.
        It's when a trend shows up in combined data, but reverses or disappears
        once you split the data into its natural groups.
        It's one of the most famous cautionary tales in statistics, and for good
        reason.
        The stakes at Berkeley were really high.
        A university's reputation was on the line, a serious accusation had been
        made in public, and it took careful, patient re-analysis to show that
        the accusation didn't hold up, even though it was built on real numbers.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-simpsons-paradox">
        <caption>A GIF Illustrating Simpson's Paradox</caption>
        <image source="images/simpsons_paradox.gif" width="100%"/>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The good news is that avoiding it doesn't need anything fancy.
        It starts with recognising how the world is actually organised, and
        keeping that organisation in your analysis, even when a single table of
        averages would be much easier to report.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bickel, P.J., Hammel, E.A.
        and O'Connell, J.W.
        (1975).
        <q>Sex Bias in Graduate Admissions: Data from Berkeley.</q>
        <em>Science</em>, 187(4175), pp.398 <ndash/> 404.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-multilevel-structures">
      <title>Multilevel Structures</title>

      <p>
        Once you know what to look for, you'll see multilevel structures
        everywhere.
        For example:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Pupils (Level 1) within classrooms (Level 2) within schools (Level
              3).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Repeated measurements (Level 1) within individuals (Level 2).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Households (Level 1) within villages (Level 2) within districts
              (Level 3).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Employees within teams within organisations.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Patients within wards within hospitals.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Then more here.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-kenya-relevance">
      <title>Why this matters here</title>

      <p>
        This is a story from 1970s America, so why are we telling it here?
        Because the same kinds of questions come up in Kenya today.
        The Kenya Universities and Colleges Central Placement Service (KUCCPS),
        which places students into university programmes, applies approved
        <term>Affirmative Action Criteria</term> covering gender, persons with
        disabilities, and marginalised regions.
        The aim is to improve access to higher education for groups who might
        otherwise be left behind (KUCCPS, n.d.).
      </p>

      <p>
        The idea behind this is one we really care about: a single overall
        number can hide important differences between groups.
        A national placement rate can tell you something useful about the system
        as a whole, and still tell you very little about how that system is
        working for particular groups of people.
        Berkeley shows exactly why this matters.
        The overall numbers told a very different story from the numbers within
        each department.
      </p>

      <p>
        We want to be careful here, though.
        Not every difference between groups is evidence of unfairness, and not
        every difference is an aggregation effect either.
        What Berkeley teaches us is that a pooled statistic is a place to start,
        and often it shouldn't be where you stop.
      </p>

      <p>
        So next time you come across a national placement rate, an exam result,
        or any other pooled statistic, ask yourself:
        <em>what groups have been combined to produce this number?</em> Then
        break the data down, look again, and only then start drawing
        conclusions.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Kenya Universities and Colleges Central Placement
        Service (KUCCPS).
        <q>Frequently Asked Questions.</q>
        <url href="https://kuccps.net/frequently-asked-questions" visual="kuccps.net/frequently-asked-questions"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch2-stack">
    <title>STACK Question Here</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Insert the STACK (ML data) question here
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch2-TODO">
      <title>TODO</title>

