      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-mics-multilevel">
    <title>Multilevel Data in R: Kenya MICS</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now let's get our hands on some real multilevel data in R.
        The Kenya Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey (MICS) is a nationally
        representative household survey, run by UNICEF and the Kenya National
        Bureau of Statistics.
        Like most modern data, it's built around a multilevel structure:
        children are nested within women, women within household members, and
        household members within households.
      </p>

      <p>
        The data is free, but you do need to register to get it.
        To follow along with the R code in this section:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Go to
              <url href="https://mics.unicef.org/surveys" visual="mics.unicef.org/surveys"/>
              and select Kenya.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Register for a free account and request access to the microdata.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Download the dataset, and put the <c>.sav</c> files in your
              working directory.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        If you're still waiting for access, don't worry.
        Read through this section anyway, because the ideas apply to any
        multilevel dataset.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-files">
      <title>Four files, four levels</title>

      <p>
        The Kenya MICS comes as four separate data files, one for each level of
        the hierarchy:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="right" width="15%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="45%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>File</cell>
          <cell>Level</cell>
          <cell>Rows</cell>
          <cell>What each row represents</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>hhKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Household</cell>
          <cell>8,993</cell>
          <cell>One household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>hlKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Household member</cell>
          <cell>46,038</cell>
          <cell>One person living in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>wmKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Woman</cell>
          <cell>10,525</cell>
          <cell>One woman aged 15 <ndash/> 49 in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>chKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Child</cell>
          <cell>7,266</cell>
          <cell>One child under 5 in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        You'll notice the row counts aren't neat multiples of each other.
        That makes sense when you think about it: not every household has a
        child under five, not every woman has children, and not every household
        member is a woman aged 15 to 49.
        Each file covers a different, overlapping group of the people in the
        survey.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can load all four files into R with the <c>haven</c> package, which
        reads SPSS <c>.sav</c> files:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(haven)
        library(dplyr)

        hh &lt;- read_sav("hhKE.sav")   # households
        hl &lt;- read_sav("hlKE.sav")   # household members
        wm &lt;- read_sav("wmKE.sav")   # women
        ch &lt;- read_sav("chKE.sav")   # children under 5

        # How many rows in each file?
        nrow(hh)
        #&gt; [1] 8993
        nrow(hl)
        #&gt; [1] 46038
        nrow(wm)
        #&gt; [1] 10525
        nrow(ch)
        #&gt; [1] 7266
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-variables">
      <title>Understanding the variable names</title>

      <p>
        Open any of the four files, and the first thing you'll notice is that
        the column names don't tell you very much.
        The household file has variables called <c>HI1</c>, <c>HI2</c>,
        <c>HI3D</c>, <c>HI3M</c>, <c>HI3Y</c>, <c>HI10</c>, <c>HIMEM</c>, and so
        on.
        This is really common in large household surveys.
        The names are short codes that are unambiguous and consistent across
        countries and survey rounds, but they tell you very little about what's
        in the variable unless you check the documentation (which is the Word
        document that comes with the Kenya MICS download).
      </p>

      <p>
        There's a good, practical reason for this.
        MICS surveys run in dozens of countries, often in several languages.
        A short code like <c>HI1</c> is language-neutral, so it's the same in
        the French and English versions of a survey.
        Long descriptive names would change from language to language, and make
        it much harder to automate comparisons across countries.
        The price you pay is that the names mean little without the
        documentation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Luckily, the Word document that comes with the Kenya MICS download gives
        you the translation.
        <c>HI1</c> is the cluster number.
        <c>HI2</c> is the household number within the cluster.
        <c>HI3D</c>, <c>HI3M</c>, and <c>HI3Y</c> are the day, month, and year
        of interview.
        <c>HI10</c> is the result of the household interview.
        <c>HIMEM</c> is the number of household members.
        Keep that document close by, because you'll need it whenever an
        unfamiliar variable name comes up (and there are a lot of them!).
      </p>

      <p>
        Even better, the <c>haven</c> package keeps the variable labels from the
        <c>.sav</c> file when you read it into R, so you don't always need to
        look them up yourself.
        The labels are attached to each column, and you can see them directly:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # See the label for one variable
      attr(hh$HI1, "label")
      #&gt; [1] "Cluster number"

      attr(hh$HIMEM, "label")
      #&gt; [1] "Number of household members"

      # See labels for all variables at once
      labelled::var_label(hh)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The screenshot below shows what this looks like if you open the data in
        the RStudio viewer (<c>View(hh)</c>).
        The short variable codes are the column headers, and the descriptive
        labels sit in a second row underneath.
        In R, the labels are stored as metadata attached to each column instead
        of as a visible row, and you can get to them whenever you need them.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-mics-variable-labels">
        <caption>
          Variable names and labels from the Kenya MICS household file, as
          displayed in a spreadsheet.
          Short codes like <c>HI1</c> and <c>HIMEM</c> are the actual column
          names; the descriptive labels below them are stored as metadata and
          preserved by the <c>haven</c> package when the file is read into R.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch2_mics_variable_labels.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A spreadsheet showing the top two rows of the Kenya MICS household
              file.
              The first row contains short variable codes: HI1, HI2, HI3D, HI3M,
              HI3Y, HI10, HIMEM.
              The second row contains descriptive labels in orange text: Cluster
              number, Household number, Day of interview, Month of interview,
              Year of interview, Result of household interview, Number of
              household members.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-linking">
      <title>How the levels connect, and where they don't</title>

      <p>
        The files are linked by a shared household identifier, <c>HID</c>.
        In theory, every record in every file should trace back to a household
        in <c>hhKE.sav</c>.
        But is that actually true?
        Let's check.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # How many unique households appear in each file?
      n_distinct(hh$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 8993
      n_distinct(hl$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 8989
      n_distinct(wm$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 7787
      n_distinct(ch$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 4955
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The household file has 8,993 households, but the household members file
        only has members from 8,989 of them.
        So there are four households with no members listed at all.
        Were they empty?
        Let's find out.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Which households are in hh but not in hl?
      missing_hids &lt;- setdiff(hh$HID, hl$HID)
      missing_hids
      #&gt; [1]      18043      76075     314034    1240005

      hh %&gt;%
        filter(HID %in% missing_hids) %&gt;%
        select(HID, HI13, HIMEM)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        This is where it gets interesting.
        Two of these four households have lots of <c>NA</c> values, including
        for the number of household members ( <c>HIMEM</c> ).
        That looks like households that were visited, but where the interview
        wasn't completed.
        The sex and age of the head of household are coded as 7 and 97, and the
        data dictionary tells us these are codes for missing values.
        So it's quite possible that nobody was at home when the survey team
        visited these households.
      </p>

      <p>
        But one of the four, household 314034, has a value for the variable that
        says there's an eligible child under 5.
        If there was a child, we'd expect to see them in the household members
        file.
        Are they in the children's file instead?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      ch %&gt;% filter(HID %in% missing_hids)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 0 x ...
      #&gt; # ... with ... variables
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        No, the child isn't in the children's file either.
        So we have a real inconsistency: the household record says there should
        be a child, but there's no record of them anywhere else.
        Was it a recording error?
        A missed interview?
        Something else?
        Honestly, we can't tell from the data alone.
      </p>

      <p>
        The women's file gives us a different puzzle.
        Household 76075 is one of our four households with no members, but it
        shows up in the women's file.
        Twice, in fact, which fits with the household record saying there are
        two women aged 15 to 49.
        Household 1240005, also missing from the household members file, has a
        woman's record too.
        So we have women in the women's file whose households have no members:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      wm %&gt;% filter(HID %in% missing_hids)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        That doesn't add up.
        A woman has to be a member of a household, so a woman's record with no
        matching household member record is a problem with the data.
        There's no way the survey design could produce it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now let's look in the other direction.
        Are there women or children whose household ID doesn't appear in the
        household file at all?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Women whose HID does not appear in the household file
      setdiff(wm$HID, hh$HID)
      #&gt; [1] ... 183 values ...

      # Children whose HID does not appear in the household file
      setdiff(ch$HID, hh$HID)
      #&gt; integer(0)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        There are 183 women whose household ID doesn't match any household in
        the household file.
        The children's file, on the other hand, is clean: every child's
        household ID is in the household file.
        So the linking problems are much bigger in the women's file than in the
        children's file.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-mics-orphans">
        <title>Real multilevel data is rarely perfectly linked</title>

        <p>
          In a perfect world, every record at every level of a multilevel
          dataset would link cleanly to exactly one record at the level above.
          In practice, this almost never happens.
          Records go missing.
          IDs get typed in wrong.
          Interviews get started and not finished.
          The four households with no members, the women with no household
          member records, and the 183 women with unmatched household IDs are all
          examples of <term>orphan records</term>: records that can't be linked
          to their parent level.
          Finding orphan records is a completely normal part of working with
          multilevel survey data, and it's really worth investigating them, like
          we did above, instead of just throwing them away.
          Sometimes an orphan record points to a coding error you can fix.
          Sometimes it shows a real gap in the data collection.
          And sometimes, like household 314034, it's an inconsistency you just
          can't resolve from the data.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        We look at how to do this kind of data cleaning in
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
        For now, the important thing is spotting these issues in the first
        place, and you can only do that once you understand the multilevel
        structure well enough to know what <q>correctly linked</q> should look
        like.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-trace">
      <title>Tracing one household through all four files</title>

      <p>
        One of our favourite exercises when meeting a multilevel dataset for the
        first time is to follow one unit all the way through every level.
        Pick a household, and find every record that relates to it across all
        four files.
        It really helps make the structure concrete.
      </p>

      <p>
        To see all four files in action, we need a household that has at least
        one woman aged 15 to 49 and at least one child under 5.
        The variables <c>HI12</c> and <c>HI13</c> in the household file give us
        these counts, so we can take the first household that has both:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      example_hid &lt;- (hh %&gt;%
        filter(HI12 &gt; 0 &amp; HI13 &gt; 0) %&gt;%
        pull(HID))[1]

      example_hid
      #&gt; [1] 2078
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Now let's pull out every record for household 2078 from each file:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Household-level record
      hh %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)

      # Everyone in the household
      hl %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 7 x ...
      # Seven people live in this household.

      # Women aged 15-49 in the household
      wm %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 2 x ...
      # Two of those seven are women aged 15-49.

      # Children under 5 in the household
      ch %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 1 x ...
      # One child under 5.
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Household 2078 has seven members.
        Two of them are women aged 15 to 49, so they each have a record in the
        women's file.
        One is a child under 5, who has a record in the children's file.
        The other four people (maybe older children, men, or elderly relatives)
        only appear in the household members file, where we can check their ages
        and sex.
        Putting the four files together, here's what we know about household
        2078:
        <ul>
          <li>
            The household record: they live in Central Kenya, and are in the 4th
            wealth quintile (where 5 is the richest).
            They have water piped into their house, and a traditional pit
            latrine.
          </li>

          <li>
            The women's records: two women live here, aged 18 and 25.
            Both have primary-level education.
            The 25-year-old has given birth, and the 18-year-old hasn't.
          </li>

          <li>
            The under-5 record: an 8-month-old girl who has had some
            vaccinations.
            The child weighs 7.2 kg and is 66 cm long.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Think about what that means for the variables.
        Household-level variables, like water source, wealth index, or
        sanitation, apply equally to all seven people in the household.
        Woman-level variables, like education or reproductive history, only
        apply to the two women.
        And child-level variables, like height, weight, or vaccination status,
        only apply to the one child.
        So if we want to ask whether this child's nutritional status is related
        to their mother's education and their household's wealth, we need to
        link across all three of those levels.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-level-mapping">
      <title>Mapping the levels</title>

      <p>
        Before we analyse any multilevel dataset, we find it really helpful to
        do a quick level-mapping exercise.
        Work out what units there are at each level, how they connect, and which
        variables are measured at each level.
        For the Kenya MICS, the map looks like this:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="55%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Level</cell>
          <cell>Unit</cell>
          <cell>Example variables measured here</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>4 (highest)</cell>
          <cell>Household</cell>
          <cell>
            Water source, Floor materials, Province, Wealth index, ...
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>Household Members</cell>
          <cell>Sex, Age, Education level, ...</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>Woman</cell>
          <cell>
            Age, Education, Number of children born, care during pregnancy, ...
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>1 (lowest)</cell>
          <cell>Child under 5</cell>
          <cell>Age in months, Health, Vaccination history, ...</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        Even this simple map raises questions that shape the analysis.
        Say we're interested in a child's nutritional status, for example
        whether they're short for their age.
        That outcome lives at Level 1.
        But the things that might explain it live at every level: the child's
        own age and sex (Level 1), their mother's education (Level 2), the
        education of other people in the household (Level 3), and the
        household's wealth, water source, and whether it's in an urban or rural
        area (Level 4).
        Leave out any one of these levels, and you risk missing something
        important.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here are the key questions we ask when mapping any multilevel dataset:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              How many units are there at each level?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What variable links each level to the one above it?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Are group sizes roughly equal, or do some groups have many more
              members than others?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What variables are measured at each level?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              At which level does the outcome of interest live, and at which
              levels do the potential explanatory variables live?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # How many household members per household?
        hl %&gt;%
          group_by(HID) %&gt;%
          summarise(n_members = n()) %&gt;%
          summarise(
            mean_size = mean(n_members),
            min_size  = min(n_members),
            max_size  = max(n_members)
          )
        #&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 3
        #&gt;   mean_size min_size max_size
        #&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1      5.12        1       23

        # How many children per household, among households
        # with at least one child?
        ch %&gt;%
          group_by(HID) %&gt;%
          summarise(n_children = n()) %&gt;%
          count(n_children)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 2
        #&gt; n_children     n
        #&gt;      &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1  3040
        #&gt; 2  1579
        #&gt; 3   286
        #&gt; 4    42
        #&gt; 5     6
        #&gt; 6     2
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        No surprises here: household sizes vary a lot, from just one member up
        to 23.
        That's typical of household survey data, and it really matters for
        analysis.
        A household with eight members contributes more rows to the dataset than
        a household with two, and if you ignore the nesting, that can distort
        even simple summaries.
        Here's an example.
        Let's calculate the mean number of rooms per household, first using the
        <c>hh</c> data, and then using the <c>hl</c> data:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mean(hh$SE4, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 2.392829
        mean(hl$SE4, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 2.507339
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The answers are different!
        The mean number of rooms is higher when we calculate it from the
        individual-level data ( <c>hl</c> ) than from the household-level data (
        <c>hh</c> ).
        That's because bigger households appear lots of times in <c>hl</c>, once
        for every household member, so their information gets repeated across
        many rows.
        If we don't account for the multilevel structure, those bigger
        households pull the mean up.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-wrong-level">
      <title>What goes wrong when you ignore the levels</title>

      <p>
        Let's say you want to find out whether urban children are better
        nourished than rural children.
        Whether a child lives in an urban or rural area depends on their
        household's cluster, so that's a cluster-level variable.
        How well nourished they are is a child-level variable.
        These two things live at different levels.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now suppose you ignore that.
        You merge the cluster variable onto every child's row, and run an
        analysis that treats every child as an independent observation.
        You'll get an answer, but it'll be overconfident.
        Children in the same cluster share a location, a local food system, a
        water supply, and local health services, so they aren't independent of
        each other.
        If you treat them as if they are, your analysis thinks it has more
        independent information than it really has, and confidence intervals
        that should be wide end up looking narrow.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the heart of the problem with multilevel structure, and it's
        exactly what multilevel methods are designed to deal with.
        Berkeley showed us one consequence of ignoring it: a conclusion that
        completely reversed once we took the groups into account.
        The MICS data shows us another: even when the conclusion doesn't flip,
        ignoring the groups gives you false precision.
        Either way, it's a really good reason to map out your levels before you
        start any analysis.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Preparing Multilevel Data for Analysis</title>

    <p>
      Before you analyse multilevel data, there are a few steps we'd really
      recommend:
      <ul>
        <li>
          Identify and check the linking keys: these are the variable(s) that
          uniquely identify each unit and link the levels together (for example,
          household ID or cluster ID).
        </li>

        <li>
          Check for orphan records: child records with no matching household
          record, or the other way round.
          These are often a sign of data entry or merging errors.
        </li>

        <li>
          Understand the sampling design: MICS surveys use complex, multi-stage
          cluster sampling.
          Your analysis needs to account for this, using survey weights and
          appropriate software (for example, R’s survey package, or the survey
          functions in R-Instat).
        </li>

        <li>
          Decide on the level of analysis: are you analysing children,
          households, or clusters?
          This decides how you need to aggregate or disaggregate your variables.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: what makes data multilevel                            -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-what-is-multilevel">
    <title>What Makes Data Multilevel</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        If you strip away the details, Berkeley and the kidney stones are really
        the same story.
        In both, individuals (applicants, patients) belong to groups
        (departments, stone sizes), and the group shapes both an individual's
        chances and how common their situation is in the data.
        Ignore the groups, and a comparison between individuals quietly turns
        into a comparison between groups, without anything telling you that's
        happened.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-levels-everywhere">
      <title>Levels are everywhere once you look for them</title>

      <p>
        And these aren't unusual datasets.
        Once you start looking for this kind of structure, you'll find it almost
        everywhere:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Pupils belong to schools, which belong to sub-counties, which
              belong to counties.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Patients belong to clinics, which belong to a county health
              system.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Farmers manage plots, which belong to a cooperative or a region.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Survey respondents belong to households, which belong to a sampled
              cluster.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        In each of these, there's more than one <term>level</term> you can
        honestly describe the data at: the individual level (a pupil, a patient,
        a plot, a person), and one or more group levels above it (a school, a
        clinic, a cooperative, a cluster).
        Calculating a number at the wrong level, or collapsing a group level
        away completely, is exactly the mistake at the heart of both stories in
        this chapter.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-multilevel-not-a-trick">
      <title>Something to check for every time</title>

      <p>
        It's tempting to think of Simpson's paradox as a rare trap that might
        catch you out once in your career, if at all.
        We really don't think that's true, and both stories in this chapter show
        why.
        Whenever your data has structure (and most real data does!), that
        structure is either going to help your analysis or quietly undermine it.
        You very rarely get to safely ignore it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The rest of this book works with data that has exactly this kind of
        structure.
        So think of this chapter as where you start building the habit: notice
        the structure, name it, and check whether it matters, before you trust a
        single overall number.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-multilevel-data">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-identify-observational-unit">
      <title>What is being measured?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the datasets below, work out the observational unit (what
          each row of the data represents), and say whether the data has a
          multilevel structure.
          If it does, describe the levels.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              A dataset from the Kenya Demographic and Health Survey, where each
              row records the age, education level, and county of residence of
              one woman interviewed in the survey.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              A dataset of exam results, where each row records the score of one
              student, along with the name of the school the student attends and
              the county the school is in.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              A dataset of daily rainfall measurements, where each row records
              the amount of rainfall at one weather station on one day, along
              with the station's location and elevation.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-aggregation-consequences">
      <title>What gets lost when you aggregate?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A researcher has data on student exam scores from 20 schools across 3
          counties in Kenya.
          They work out the average score across all the students in the
          dataset, and reports it as the county average.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What have they assumed, without saying so, by calculating a single
              average across all the students?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example of a situation where this assumption would lead
              them to the wrong conclusion.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What would they need to do differently to get a valid county-level
              average?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-simpsons-paradox-spot">
      <title>Spotting the hidden variable</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A hospital reports that its overall patient survival rate went up from
          72% in 2020 to 78% in 2022.
          A journalist writes a story praising the hospital for improving its
          care.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then a data analyst breaks the survival rate down by ward, and finds
          this:
        </p>

        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Ward</cell>
            <cell>2020 survival rate</cell>
            <cell>2022 survival rate</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>General</cell>
            <cell>90%</cell>
            <cell>88%</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Intensive Care</cell>
            <cell>50%</cell>
            <cell>48%</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Survival rates went down in both wards between 2020 and 2022.
              So how could the overall survival rate go up?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What's the hidden variable that the journalist's story ignored?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What does this example have in common with the Berkeley admissions
              case from earlier in this chapter?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multilevel-kuccps">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-kenya-relevance"/> mentions that KUCCPS applies
          Affirmative Action Criteria covering gender when placing students into
          degree programmes.
          Suppose a single national placement rate showed a gap between men and
          women.
          If you think about it the way the Berkeley statisticians did, what
          grouping variable would you want that national rate broken down by,
          before deciding whether the gap was down to unfair treatment or to
          differences in <em>where</em> people applied?
          What pattern in that breakdown would tell you the gap was a result of
          Simpson's paradox, rather than a real difference?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multilevel-kidney-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In your own words, explain why the newer kidney stone procedure looked
          better overall, but worse within every group defined by stone size.
          Try to explain <em>which</em> patients were more likely to get which
          treatment, and go further than saying the numbers <q>reversed</q>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>